      "title": "How to apply",
      "description": "Job application guide - University of Canberra <iframe src=\"https://www.googletagmanager.com/ns.html?id=GTM-PRQLQP\" height=\"0\" width=\"0\" style=\"display:none;visibility:hidden\"> Study Accommodation Accommodation Guarantee Other housing options Resolving disputes About About UC Library Library facilities Research Research equipment MyUC UC account Skip navigation University of Canberra home page Menu Future students Toggle submenu Future students Find a course Find a course Study at UC Browse study areas Online courses UC College International Parents and carers guide Life and living at UC Student support Entry requirements and pathways Scholarships Australian Indigenous students Apply Start at UC Talk to us Careers advisors and agents Research Toggle submenu Research Discover more About UC research Research Plan 2024 - 2028 Research impact Life of a CBRn Research centres and institutes Contribute to research Graduate Research School Research news Contact us Give Toggle submenu Give Discover more Student Support Appeal 2026 Funds you can support How to give Impact of giving Contact us On campus Toggle submenu On campus Discover more Campus tours UC Collections Facilities Campus Master Plan Venue hire Campus cafes and shops Childcare Health and support Transport Parking Adobe Creative Campus Sport at UC Security Lost and found property Safe community Report an incident About UC Toggle submenu About UC Discover more University strategy Senior management Faculties Campus locations Educational partners Graduation Office of Indigenous Leadership Academic integrity Sustainability Media and communication Governance Policy and legislation Learning at UC Learning and Teaching Student Equity and Participation Equity and diversity Work at UC Key dates Contact University of Canberra Safe community Report an incident Students Staff Alumni Library Maps Safety & Support Search Search Search Safety & Support Accessibility and language Students Staff Alumni Library Maps Safety & Support UC Open Day Fest - For future students, families, and the whole community. Saturday 12 September, 10am to 3pm UC Open Day Fest - Saturday 12 September, 10am to 3pm UC Open Day Fest Sat 12 Sept, 10am–3pm REGISTER NOW How to apply Home / About UC / Work at UC / How to apply Work at UC Jobs at UC Flexibility and leave benefits Financial and corporate benefits Workplace culture and wellbeing Training and career development How to apply Job application FAQs Interested in working at the University of Canberra? Explore how to apply to UC and discover why our people are at the centre of everything we do. Together, we work to empower, connect, and share experiences with our people, cultures and places. Our people bring their unique perspectives to UC through multiple factors of their identities. We embrace this diversity of background, skills, experiences and knowledge. Is a selection criteria and cover letter required? The University of Canberra does not ask for selection criteria or cover letters. Instead, we ask you to ensure you take the time to align your Resume with the Position Description requirements (which will be linked to the job you apply for). We want to understand how your skills and experience relate to the position you are applying for. In very rare circumstances we may ask for a cover letter, but most of our roles will only ask you to apply with your Resume. What is my eligibility to apply? To be considered for roles at the University of Canberra, applicants are required to have full working rights in Australia at the time of application. If you have any working restrictions, please indicate this as part of your application. The University of Canberra generally will not offer sponsorship except in very specific circumstances, subject to government Labour Market Testing, and is at the university’s discretion Learn more about working visas . Pre-employment security check Working rights (VEVO check) Working with Vulnerable People (WWVP) registration (ACT) (pending role) National Police check (pending role) Qualification (pending role) Industry registration (pending role) How long does the recruitment process take? Recruitment process timeframes can vary for multiple reasons. Our advertisements will vary between 1, 2 or 4 weeks and we aim for the process to take 2 to 4 weeks after the advertised closing date for the panel to follow the process steps below. Review all applications Shortlist applications Video interviews Interviews References (generally 2 are required, 1 of which would need to be a direct manager) Pre-employment checks Verbal offer Formal contract We aim to contact all applicants with an outcome or next steps from their application within 2 to 3 weeks of the advertisement close date. At any point in the recruitment process, applicants are welcome to contact UC Talent for an update on the recruitment process if it has exceeded the outlined timeline. Please note that all recruitment process vary to an extent, and we consider the availability of applicants and panel members which can often change the expected timelines. What happens after I'm offered a role at UC? Once you receive an offer of employment and have a confirmed start date, one of our Employee Experience Coordinators (EECO) will contact you to go through your onboarding process with you. They will organise your ID card, university tour, and relocation assistance, and will assist you in organising your onboarding training and experience. Can I send in an Expression of Interest? We have five faculties at UC and each faculty manages their own sessional and casual talent pool. You can apply to each faculty separately by submitting an expression of interest for each one. Don't see the right opportunity for you? If you do not see a suitable role for you to apply to, we encourage you to subscribe to our Job Alerts and we'll notify you of upcoming opportunities of interest. Please note, you will only be contacted if a suitable opportunity arises. How do I ask for adjustments in the application and recruitment process? UC is committed to accessibility to all. If you require a reasonable adjustment, please contact the UC Talent team and we will aim to assist with your request. If you are not based in Canberra or are unable to attend the interview in person, you are welcome to request a virtual interview via Teams. Does UC have culturally safe recruitment practices? At the University of Canberra, we strive to provide a culturally safe experience through the application and selection processes. We are committed to creating an inclusive environment full of different perspectives, and we actively work towards building a diverse and inclusive community where everyone feels a sense of belonging. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates UC encourages applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates. The ambitious goals of our Indigenous Leadership Strategy 2024- 2026 are designed to ensure that our Indigenous students and staff flourish in a welcoming and culturally safe environment. Who should I contact if I have questions on a job I'm interested in? If you have a question about the job you are applying for, the hiring manager’s details will be listed on the job advert. If your questions relate to your application or the recruitment process, please contact the UC Talent team. +61 2 6206 3867 0447 404 529 uctalent@canberra.edu.au When do I get feedback after my interview? The hiring manager will contact all applicants that have attended a video or face-to-face interview within 2 to 3 weeks of attendance. For any queries about the recruitment process,  please contact the UC Talent team. +61 2 6206 3867 0447 404 529 uctalent@canberra.edu.au Interview and CV tips Resume tips We encourage all applicants to tailor their resume to align to the role they have applied for. Realistically for those with a number of years of experience, it can be hard to include all of your skills and achievements on a few pages. This is where reviewing the position description to ensure you have highlighted and included your most suited skills to the role can stand you apart from other applicants. Sample resume / CV Utilise a chronological format for your CV, emphasising detailed information from the past ten years of your career. For experiences spanning ten years or longer, provide only essential information. Ideally a resume should not be more than 2 – 5 pages in length.  If you are an academic, consider adding your publications after the initial three pages. Contact details Name Location (your city or suburb or residence is enough for your CV) Mobile/Home phone (please include area code in case you are out of state or overseas) Email address: Summary The summary should be an overview of who you are, what you are looking for and what are you seeking in your next opportunity. Education and qualifications Qualifications Institution Year completed Employment history Position title: Department/Organisation Tenure (start to end date. Month and year is sufficient): Responsibilities List your main responsibilities Achievements List your notable achievements here References Will be provided on request. (This ensures your references are current.) Interview tips University of Canberra has many exciting opportunities, and we look forward to inviting shortlisted applicants to meet with us to interview. Each recruitment process may vary from one another depending on the role and its requirements. We would like to you invite you to review some of our top tips for interviewing at UC and what to expect. Once you have submitted your application and invited to proceed to the next stage of the process, you may be invited to go through a one-way video interview first or to meet with the panel for a face to face (or online/Teams) interview. Our interview panel are comprised of at least 3 panel member with a composition of the hiring manager, a gender balance, and a panel member outside of the direct work area. We pride ourselves on our approach to diversity of thought and providing all applicants with a fair evaluation. Our interviews range between 30 minutes to one hour depending the role. You will be asked a mix of soft skills and technical skills. In some cases, you may be asked to engage in an interview skills task (this could be during your interview or a task to be completed prior to your interview showcasing your skills and ability relevant to the role you have applied to). The UC Talent team will advise you prior to your interview if a task is requested to be prepared. Be authentic, open and honest. We want to get to know you and invite an open dialogue as part of your interview. We invite you to read about our values to ensure you are aligned with our mission and culture UC is passionate about diversity, inclusion, equity, and accessibility. If you require a reasonable adjustment made to your interview, please contact the UC Talent team. +61 2 6206 3867 0447 404 529 uctalent@canberra.edu.au Video interview tips UC uses one-way video interviewing as a screening tool before a face-to-face or Teams interview with a panel. Many of our positions will request video interviews. However, this may vary depending on the role you are applying for and the volume of applications. What to expect on one-way video interview Upon receiving the one-way interview link, log in and explore the platform. Test for any technical issues, ensuring both sound and camera are functioning properly. Give yourself practice sessions to familiarise yourself with the system; repeat as needed before you go live. Avoid delaying the interview to the last minute to prevent any unforeseen issues. For technical assistance, please use the 24/7 chat box on the platform (this is not an automated robot and you will have a real person to assist you with any issues). Select a quiet, interruption-free environment for the interview. Choose a professional background, such as a plain wall or curtain. Dress in a manner consistent with in-person professional standards. Maintain eye contact with the camera when providing your answers. Keep notes on hand if needed during the interview. Thoroughly read and adhere to the provided instructions. Be mindful of time constraints for both reading the question and answering. Some questions may allow multiple attempts, while others offer only one chance to respond. Keep your responses concise and to the point. Stop the clock once you've completed your answer and move on to the next question. View our full terms and conditions of employment in our University of Canberra Enterprise Agreement 2023-2026 (PDF) Talk to us If you have questions about a specific job advertised, please get in touch with the contact person listed on the advertisement. For all other enquiries related to your career with us, please get in touch with our UC Talent team on 02 6206 3867 or email uctalent@canberra.edu.au Email us Other quick links Also of interest Career connections Work at UC Jobs at UC Contact us Future students Current students Alumni contact us Donors General enquiries Faculties Arts and Design Business, Government and Law Education Health Science and Technology News and events UnCover Newsroom Find an expert Events Blog Services Security Library Medical and Counselling IT Service updates Gym Accommodation Administration Maps Job opportunities Staff directory Researcher profiles Freedom of information Policies and procedures University of Canberra, Bruce ACT 2617 Australia +61 2 6201 5111 ABN 81 633 873 422 CRICOS 00212K TEQSA Provider ID: PRV12003 (Australian University) Contact UC Copyright Disclaimer Privacy Accessibility Collection notice UC acknowledges the Ngunnawal people, traditional custodians of the lands where Bruce campus is situated. We wish to acknowledge and respect their continuing culture and the contribution they make to the life of Canberra and the region. We also acknowledge all other First Nations Peoples on whose lands we gather."
